    {
      "id": "1455888340393136276",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2025-12-31T06:40:29.447-05:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "I recently drove for about an hour using the latest WMI model (3547a2cc-1699-4e7d-a2ab-4eb87d0b8684) in experimental (E2E) mode, and the best word I can find to describe it is “magic.”\n\nThe lateral control is outstanding. Lane centering feels extremely stable and confident — almost like driving with a very experienced human driver who naturally knows how to stay perfectly centered.\n\nOn the longitudinal side, the behavior is impressively human-like. When the model detects vehicles waiting at a traffic light further ahead, it starts slowing down earlier than previous versions, which feels smooth and anticipatory rather than reactive. Acceleration from a stop is also very responsive, and I find myself needing to press the accelerator far less often than before. Overall, it feels calm, natural, and thoughtful.\n\nThat said, there is one area where I think this otherwise excellent model still has room to grow. In urban driving, it struggles to maintain speed. Even when I manually accelerate, it tends to slow down again, causing it to fall behind the vehicle ahead. This is especially noticeable on wide, four-lane roads: when the adjacent lane is congested but my own lane is clear, the car seems to match the slower neighboring lane instead of accelerating toward the configured maximum speed. When my lane is open, I would love to see the model confidently accelerate closer to the set speed.",
      "author": {
        "id": "376928180692320258",
        "name": ".nickwilde",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "NickWilde",
        "color": "#979C9F",
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [
          {
            "id": "894068204664676373",
            "name": "Community Members",
            "color": "#979C9F",
            "position": 56
          },
          {
            "id": "1014908932856225943",
            "name": "Toyota, Lexus",
            "color": "#11806A",
            "position": 45
          },
          {
            "id": "1274624610255638621",
            "name": "comma 3X",
            "color": "#1F8B4C",
            "position": 29
          }
        ],
        "avatarUrl": "https://cdn.discordapp.com/avatars/376928180692320258/7d0f728c3e4f9414354eb5e6c58d45c3.png?size=512"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
      "reactions": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1456002427726594120",
      "type": "Reply",
      "timestamp": "2025-12-31T14:13:49.987-05:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "So you hyped me up enough to try Experimental on my way back, and I have to say wow. Best lateral and longitudinal performance I’ve ever had on my Atlas.\n\nOn the downside, it seems to want to roll through red lights. It stops initially, but then starts creeping forward. Interestingly, I didn’t have this issue at stop signs.\n\nAnd as all the other models I tried, following distance and speed compliance are still pretty rough. This model is much better at taking off and getting up to the speed limit, but then it’ll sit anywhere from 5–10 mph under while you watch the lead car slowly disappear \uD83D\uDE02. I accelerated to catch back up, and when I let off, it went a little too hard on the brakes even though I was roughly 4 cars lengths behind. At this point it started going around 20mph slower than what the lead car was doing. (He was easily going  10 over). I hope that comma eventually fixes this. FSD had the same problem so it’s not new to me.",
      "author": {
        "id": "476880092895641601",
        "name": "pelonetta",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Pelonetta",
        "color": "#979C9F",
